## Solution

**Coordinate setup.** Place $B=(0,0)$, $C=(15,0)$. From $AB=13$, $AC=14$:
$$x_A^2+y_A^2=169,\quad (x_A-15)^2+y_A^2=196 \implies x_A=\tfrac{33}{5},\ y_A=\tfrac{56}{5}.$$
So $A=(33/5,\,56/5)$.

**Angle bisector foot $L$.** By the angle-bisector theorem, $BL:LC=13:14$, hence
$$L=\Big(\tfrac{15\cdot 13}{27},0\Big)=\Big(\tfrac{65}{9},0\Big).$$

**Circumcenter $O_1$ of $\triangle ABL$.** Since $B,L$ lie on the $x$-axis, the perpendicular bisector of $BL$ is $x=65/18$. The perpendicular bisector of $AB$ (midpoint $(33/10,28/5)$, slope of $AB$ is $56/33$, so perpendicular slope $-33/56$) gives, at $x=65/18$:
$$y=\tfrac{28}{5}-\tfrac{33}{56}\Big(\tfrac{65}{18}-\tfrac{33}{10}\Big)=\tfrac{28}{5}-\tfrac{33}{56}\cdot\tfrac{14}{45}=\tfrac{28}{5}-\tfrac{11}{60}=\tfrac{65}{12}.$$
Thus $O_1=(65/18,\,65/12)$.

**Circumcenter $O_2$ of $\triangle ACL$.** The perpendicular bisector of $CL$ is $x=100/9$. The perpendicular bisector of $AC$ (slope of $AC$ is $-4/3$, perpendicular slope $3/4$, midpoint $(54/5,28/5)$) gives, at $x=100/9$:
$$y=\tfrac{28}{5}+\tfrac{3}{4}\Big(\tfrac{100}{9}-\tfrac{54}{5}\Big)=\tfrac{28}{5}+\tfrac{3}{4}\cdot\tfrac{14}{45}=\tfrac{28}{5}+\tfrac{7}{30}=\tfrac{35}{6}.$$
Thus $O_2=(100/9,\,35/6)$.

**Foot $B_1$ from $C$ to the bisector of $\angle ABC$.** Unit vectors along $BA$ and $BC$ are $(33,56)/65$ and $(1,0)$, so the bisector direction is $(98,56)\propto(7,4)$; the bisector line is $4x-7y=0$. Projecting $C=(15,0)$:
$$B_1=\Big(15-\tfrac{4\cdot 60}{65},\,0+\tfrac{7\cdot 60}{65}\Big)=\Big(\tfrac{147}{13},\tfrac{84}{13}\Big).$$

**Foot $C_1$ from $B$ to the bisector of $\angle ACB$.** Unit vectors along $CA$ and $CB$ are $(-3/5,4/5)$ and $(-1,0)$, so the bisector direction is $(-8,4)\propto(-2,1)$; the bisector line is $x+2y-15=0$. Projecting $B=(0,0)$:
$$C_1=\Big(0-\tfrac{1\cdot(-15)}{5},\,0-\tfrac{2\cdot(-15)}{5}\Big)=(3,6).$$

**Concurrency point $P$ on $BC$ (the $x$-axis).** Line $O_1C_1$: direction $C_1-O_1=(-11/18,\,7/12)$. Setting $y=0$:
$$\tfrac{65}{12}+\tfrac{7t}{12}=0\implies t=-\tfrac{65}{7},\qquad x=\tfrac{65}{18}+\tfrac{65}{7}\cdot\tfrac{11}{18}=\tfrac{65}{18}\cdot\tfrac{18}{7}=\tfrac{65}{7}.$$
So $P=(65/7,\,0)$.

**Verification with line $O_2B_1$.** Direction $B_1-O_2=(23/117,\,49/78)$. Setting $y=0$:
$$\tfrac{35}{6}+\tfrac{49t}{78}=0\implies t=-\tfrac{65}{7},\qquad x=\tfrac{100}{9}-\tfrac{65}{7}\cdot\tfrac{23}{117}=\tfrac{9100-1495}{819}=\tfrac{7605}{819}=\tfrac{65}{7}.\ \checkmark$$

Both lines meet $BC$ at the same point, confirming $P=(65/7,0)$.

**Computing $AP$.**
$$AP^2=\Big(\tfrac{65}{7}-\tfrac{33}{5}\Big)^2+\Big(\tfrac{56}{5}\Big)^2=\Big(\tfrac{94}{35}\Big)^2+\Big(\tfrac{56}{5}\Big)^2=\tfrac{8836}{1225}+\tfrac{153664}{1225}=\tfrac{162500}{1225}=\tfrac{6500}{49}.$$
$$AP=\frac{\sqrt{6500}}{7}=\frac{10\sqrt{65}}{7}.$$

## Answer

$$\boxed{AP=\dfrac{10\sqrt{65}}{7}}$$

